{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1793495300,"nonce":"DSqBKerfoBoR8B9JFijN5fNeoRE=","title":"A visible spinner repaints the whole tree every frame when a cross-stream group is cached","message":"cn_demo sat at 25-33% CPU per window once the Loading States section was on screen, and at 0% with it scrolled away. Every frame held by the three `cn::spinner`s took the full paint path: about 14 ms of main-thread time per frame (paint walker, `collect_elements_recursive`, image passes), at about 27-30 fps.\n\nCause. The spinners are promoted motion subtrees (P4.3): each arc is baked into a layer texture once and `composite_motion_layers_overlay` blits it at the current rotation, so a fast frame needs no walk. The windowed loop did ask for the fast path (`fast: true`), and the compositor took it (`use_fast`, static layer valid). Then `apply_binding_deltas` returned false on every frame, from the cross-stream guard added in 4b8c042675cc24b310bc54c02f85374019ddb9b1: any motion binding in the tree plus any cached batch with cross-stream groups rejects the patch. A rejected patch invalidates the static layer and the collected-content caches, and the inner canvas splice rejects through the same function, so the walker runs.\n\nOn cn_demo the dynamic batch always holds cross-stream groups: the two empty overlay roots are portal groups, and every node with opacity below one over text or SVG (disabled controls, hidden check icons) plans a content group that makes each ancestor a group too, up to the root. The spinners themselves had no `composite_bindings` entry (they paint into a composite scratch batch), so nothing in the cached batches needed patching.\n\nRuled out: switch transitions (end in about 0.5 s and release the scheduler); `Device::poll(Wait)` per frame. Making that poll non-blocking raised CPU to about 72%, because the wait was pacing frames; the per-frame cost was the walk.\n\nFix. The walker records whether it painted a motion-bound node outside a promoted composite layer (`RenderTree::painted_unpromoted_binding`). When the tree has motion bindings, none was painted outside a promoted layer and no composite binding was recorded, `apply_binding_deltas` accepts without patching. Moving captures (a bound node inside a cross-stream group cannot promote), foreground-only bindings and recorded composite bindings still reach the existing guards and repaint.\n\nAfter, same scroll position (dev profile, optimized): every frame takes the fast path, render step median about 0.55 ms (was about 14.2 ms), process CPU about 12-15% at about 50-55 fps (was about 23% at about 30 fps). Main thread about 4% busy; the rest is the wake timer, Metal completion and frame pacing. Scrolled away: no frames, 0%. Captures across fast frames show the spinners turning.\n\nTest: `context::tests::motion_layers_tests::promoted_rotation_beside_a_cross_stream_group_takes_the_patch_path` (GPU, ignored by default) renders a promoted rotating bar beside a translucent group, asserts the patch is accepted, and that the fast frame at 90 degrees matches a full paint pixel for pixel and differs from the 0 degree frame. It fails without the fix. `static_motion_group_public_keeps_overlap_isolated_during_binding_updates` still pins the moving-capture rejection, and `foreground_layers_cache_patchers_request_full_paint_without_mutation` the foreground guard.\n\nRemaining cost: three spinners still present at vsync. A per-animation frame cap would lower it further; not attempted.","files":null}]}